                 =================  SSH (Secure Shell) ===================

SSH (Secure Shell) uses asymmetric cryptography to securely log into one server from another without a password. It works via two linked keys:

. Public Key: Actively distributed to the Remote Server. It acts like a digital padlock.
. Private Key: Kept securely on the Local Server. It serves as the physical key that opens the padlock

=>> Step-by-Step Connection Guide:
Let's assume you want to connect from Server A (Local) to Server B (Remote).

1. Generate Key Pair on Server ALog into Server A and generate a strong cryptographic key pair:

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "serverA_connection"
. -t ed25519: Specifies the modern, highly secure Ed25519 encryption algorithm.
. Press Enter to accept the default storage location (~/.ssh/id_ed25519).
. Choose whether to enter an optional password (passphrase) to protect the key file itself

This action creates two distinct files in your ~/.ssh/ directory:
A. id_ed25519 (Your confidential Private Key)
B.  id_ed25519.pub (Your sharable Public Key)


2. Copy the Public Key to Server B

To share the "padlock" with Server B, execute the OpenSSH built-in copy tool:

ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub user@server_b_ip

Note: 
. You will be asked to enter Server B’s password one final time to authorize the transfer.
. This command safely appends the public key to Server B's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys file.

3. Establish the Connection
Connect seamlessly from Server A to Server B without typing a password
ssh -i  .ssh/id_ed25519 user@server_b_ip

